Executor span — the stage that changes the cluster was untraced on every real path
===================================================================================
Date : 2026-07-28
Mode : real uvicorn webhook + real OTLP/gRPC export (Docker down → local OTLP sink
       speaking the collector wire protocol, in place of Tempo)
Gate : make check 1446 -> 1454 (+8)


The recorded gap was narrower than the real one
-------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PLAN carried "② executor span (선택) — 현재 parking 경로만 실증. 승인 후 실행
경로는 미측정." True, and the reason turned out to cover more than the approval
path.

`run_incident_pipeline(execute=True)` was traced, and that is what the existing
tests exercised. Nothing reaches the pipeline that way in production: the webhook
— the On-Prem PATH B entry point — calls it with `execute=False` and executes
afterwards, on **both** the AUTO path and the approval path. Both executions
happened *after* the pipeline's root span had already closed.

So the one stage that actually changes the cluster produced **no span at all on
every path a real alert takes**. The tests could not see it because they entered
one layer below the gap — the same shape as the runbook tier finding earlier
today, and as every "declared but unreached" defect this repo has paid for.


What was built
---------------
- The span moved *into* `execute_incident`, so all three call sites get it from
  one implementation rather than one of three being instrumented.
- `onprem.webhook.incident` root span, so the AUTO path's execution shares a
  trace with the detect/analyze/decide legs that chose it instead of being an
  orphan.
- `onprem.webhook.approve` root span for the approval path, and a **span link**
  back to the incident — not a parent. The interval between them is a human
  deciding; folding that into the incident's span tree would produce a span whose
  duration is mostly someone reading Slack, and every latency figure derived from
  the trace would be wrong.
- `SpanOrigin` (trace id + span id) persisted with the parked approval, because a
  trace id alone cannot form a link. Written only when present — a record parked
  with tracing off must not look like one whose origin was lost.
- A half-known origin yields **no link**, only an attribute. An invalid link
  renders as a dead reference, which is the failure `current_trace_id` already
  refuses to produce ("show no trace link rather than a dead one").


