Live: approval path — two traces, one link
--------------------------------------------
P2 parked (`APR-6CEF3B57`), stored origin:
    {'trace_id': '110e6741f58c7adab12ad0264d4705f5', 'span_id': 'acc625d5b11365fd'}

    TRACE 110e6741f58c7ada…   root=onprem.webhook.incident
      onprem.webhook.incident     5345.9ms
      onprem.incident_pipeline    5345.2ms   (span acc625d5)
      onprem.analyze              4932.8ms
      onprem.decide                411.9ms
      onprem.detect                  0.3ms
      -- no execute span: nothing ran, and claiming otherwise would be a lie

    TRACE 7ebba25008dc5502…   root=onprem.webhook.approve
      onprem.webhook.approve       932.2ms
      onprem.execute               932.2ms
        └─ LINK → trace 110e6741f58c7ada… span acc625d5
        └─ attr executor.origin_trace_id=110e6741f58c7ada…

The link resolves to the exact span that proposed the remediation. The ~11s of
human decision time appears in neither trace's durations — which is the entire
argument for a link over a parent.

Executed for real: `ONPREM-RolloutRestartWorkload`, INC-615A4BEB, resolved=true.


Falsification
--------------
  - execute span removed (the original state)      -> 5 failures
  - approval executes without the origin link      -> 1 failure (the link test)
  - webhook root span removed                      -> 1 failure (AUTO shares one trace)
Restoring passed all 8.

Also fixed while here: `span()` took attributes via `**kwargs`, and OTel keys are
dotted (`executor.resolved`) so callers wrote `**{"a.b": v}`. A `**` unpack can
collide with the named parameters — an attribute called "link" would have
silently become a span link. Attributes now pass as a dict; the keyword form
still works.


WHAT IS STILL OPEN
-------------------
- **The four newly-selectable runbooks do not match on-prem alerts.** Surfaced by
  this run: a disk-filling alert resolved to `generic-recovery`. The on-prem
  detector normalises every Alertmanager alert to
  `namespace=ONPREM/kubernetes-workload`, `metric_name=availability`, so the
  alertname (`NodeFilesystemSpaceFillingUp`) never reaches the matcher, which
  scores on namespace + metric text. Those runbooks are keyed on CloudWatch
  namespaces and metric names, so on the on-prem path they can only match via
  root-cause text. Not a regression — they were unselectable everywhere before —
  but it means "selectable" currently means "selectable on the AWS path".
  Whether on-prem alertnames should feed the matcher is a matching-design
  question, left rather than invented.
- Tempo itself was not used: Docker was down, so the export went over real
  OTLP/gRPC to a local sink implementing the collector's TraceService. Wire
  protocol and protobuf decoding are exercised; Tempo's own ingestion is not.
